#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:5
#: 4bb1698eedc9411a94a8f2799c9d7df4
msgid "Building a Blimp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:8
#: d13aeec2642148b9a1465c1e8fa5eba1
msgid "Building an Indoor finned Blimp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:9
#: 99c09743e10f47c496955dd69ffdc9e3
msgid "This type of blimp has four flapping fins driven by servos, which combine to provide X, Y, Z and Yaw control, while pendulum stability keeps roll and pitch close to zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:11
#: e0b49ad02557458cae2432745bcf600b
msgid "These instructions are specifically for building a ~50cm diameter spherical blimp, although the sizes of parts can be adapted to build bigger blimps of the same type. This size of balloon will give about 50g of lift (excluding the lift required to lift the envelope itself)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:14
#: c6bcb6e8685a4ca9b43dd6165ff1e816
msgid "Parts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:16
#: 4a731730dfc34061b5b2657555bb5c08
msgid "Autopilot. This is the most complex part to find due to the very specific limitations. Due to the very low weight limit, none of the usual high-end autopilots are suitable, as they are much too heavy. The most suitable autopilots are the single-pcb type, such as the Matek F405-STD, which has had extensive testing with Blimp. It includes a microSD card slot and has good rc input and pwm outputs, which make it highly convenient, especially for development, but it still weighs 9 grams. Using a autopilot like this one which is designed to be powered from 5v rather than battery power is optimal, as autopilots that can be powered from battery usually require a minimum of 2S or 3S voltage, and the only servos small enough for this application can only run from 5v or less. There are some other autopilots available which weigh closer to 2-3 grams which would allow for more payload, although they generally have a dataflash chip instead of a microSD card slot (among other things) which is less convenient. :ref:`This page <common-autopilots>` gives some further information on what to consider in an ArduPilot autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:17
#: 0d96b881d58f4354993bb2447ef7ac4f
msgid "Envelope to be filled with helium. The ~50 cm spherical foil party balloons work quite well for indoor use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:18
#: 540e466d31cb42d188b427c7045866c0
msgid "Four servos. The 1.7g micro servos are suggested."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:19
#: 65f65a07b32b4f6cb1f6e7684810f021
msgid "Approx. 1-2mm thick sheets of balsa wood and plastic freezer bags for making the fins."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:20
#: 6acbf5b4d8ec48c4bd763c28483d4ca3
msgid "Very thin wires."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:21
#: 504b86ef2d854fd6a6095a537949b406
msgid "Battery. For this size of blimp, 150-300mAh 1S is generally most suitable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:22
#: 9c501b203b6d4fd69b0007baadb715f8
msgid "Telemetry module. Unfortunately, the usual SiK telemetry modules are too heavy for this case. The smallest versions of the ESP8266 modules, such as the ESP-01 or ESP-07 are most suitable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:23
#: 6f619d9ca36f4fdbaab4f5304303b3be
msgid "For RC control, again, finding a tiny RC receiver is necessary. The OrangeRx Nano DSM2/DSMX receiver is a great option as it only weighs 0.5 grams."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:24
#: bab4b893f1734b77a677bd3d1ae42b50
msgid "A 5V Step-Up Voltage Regulator for powering the autopilot from 1S. In tests it was found that the autopilot could be powered from the battery directly, however it browned out at around 3.6v (i.e. rebooted repeatedly due to low voltage) which means you would not be able to use the full battery capacity if you do not use one of these."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:27
#: 36ff59ed662c4bd280974aea6cb40880
msgid "Wiring"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:29
#: 65e345e1e2e84fc0a160a16079bd31ce
msgid "The 5v step-up converter is added to allow the autopilot to run from a 1S battery. Its input comes from directly from the battery, and the output goes to the autopilot's 5v pin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:31
#: 7fb230c1c1ed49ffa75c45362704d8a4
msgid "If a MatekF405-STD autopilot is used, the RC receiver is powered from one of the 4.5v pins on the autopilot. Otherwise, any 5v pin on the board can be used. This is because the 4.5v pins are also powered when the autopilot is powered via USB, thus allowing setup and RC calibration without a battery connected. The receiver's output is connected to the RX2 input of the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:33
#: 72783c6318fd4699aa229fa1d3e66fd4
msgid "The Wi-Fi module is powered directly from the battery. Its TX and RX pins are connected to the RX3 and TX3 pins on the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:35
#: 9a9b4a45285f4cc58a5d001bbf386312
msgid "The four servos are connected to four of the servo/motor outputs on the board, and are powered directly from the battery."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:38
#: b5c72fa9c55448f69a354c5dc2005952
msgid "Building the Fins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:40
#: 437d8208d7f04bf594a4463d94c77ddd
msgid "Cut 8 strips from the sheet of balsa wood, measuring 185 mm by 5 mm. Make sure to cut along the grain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:41
#: 1dadab05f5934fbfb0610e6f1c7b813f
msgid "Use super glue to glue the ends of two strips at 90 degrees to each other, forming a right-angled triangle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:42
#: 3e035e9ebd1e45848a03250b5a54e7a7
msgid "Repeat this with the other 6 strips."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:43
#: ab6d78aee42b458995ddbf4673371bca
msgid "Cut triangles out of the plastic of the same size as the wood frames."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:44
#: 25fb0bb79ac34e68870f3a681552713e
msgid "Glue the plastic to the the wood frames. The hypotenuse edge will be left loose. Try to keep the plastic pulled flat while you glue, but without warping the wood frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:45
#: e70c7fd0332940a9b6553699461c8ab7
msgid "Glue the 90 deg attachment points to the corner of the triangle where you glued the two strips together."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:47
#: b02f168296f44176bdd31b45909c294f
msgid "See the photo below for an example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:54
#: 636afcb609dd4fe396f859abf0d6c79e
msgid "Putting it all together"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:56
#: e4c1afd370b144ebb3c603c57e1fbe84
msgid "The servos need to be attached in a specific orientation in order for the flapping with offset to work correctly. These are as follows, orientation is looking directly at the servo and balloon from 90 degrees:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:59
#: 610bfbd345d340a09cd61e397a308172
msgid "The front and back servos are oriented sideways so that the fin will flap up and down when the servo moves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:60
#: 759591db2b084598ac7b9b91eb96edc8
msgid "Front servo: output gear at the top left of the servo body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:61
#: 1201222916954861b7ab9c85bd2ae12f
msgid "Back servo: output gear at the top right of the servo body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:62
#: ca20837fb3564f96a0ed44678e6af51d
msgid "The left and right servos are oriented upright so that the fin will flap left to right when the servo moves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:63
#: 8a3664fd704f4e9a8b6173b6f48de9d5
msgid "Left servo: output gear at the top right of the servo body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:64
#: 2eb7c5aa4ace45248715312639aa348a
msgid "Right servo: output gear at the top left of the servo body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:66
#: 36a5056bf2114749b92a96f119a66036
msgid "See the photos below for the orientation of each servo:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:77
#: 299f68c3bf0d4e96a1c7558b711ef5b5
msgid "Back fin servo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:89
#: 97eaf45ceb704b4eb86cd6a8ca4bba29
msgid "Front fin servo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:99
#: c74734f1dbde474a862a68d79dcb4aa1
msgid "Left fin servo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:111
#: 2c80b8cef36c4783b3e537059def94c6
msgid "Right fin servo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/building-a-blimp.rst:119
#: 660c0e2b723b4fff8664d028dfcb9d07
msgid "Now the servo horns can be glued onto the fins to allow for attaching to the servos. Take care when gluing them so that there is enough clearance between the fin and the servo's body to allow for its full range of motion. With the fins and servos in the photos, it was best to glue the servo horns such that when attached to the servo, the wood of the fin is on the side of the servo where the output gear is closer to the edge."
msgstr ""
